the villagers of taparto in northwest  colombia used their bare hands to search  for the children trapped under a  mountain of felled trees and mud a  landslide buried this rural school in a  matter of seconds 22 students were  inside  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  three of them died two six-year-old  girls and one five-year-old boy  a father hopelessly hugging one of them  the other 19 were found alive when  rescue teams arrived with earth-moving  equipment  the national army the national police  and rescue officers are helping this  educational institution where a  landslide caught young students unaware  as they were taking class inside as you  can see we are using excavators to help  with the emergency  armed forces are at the top helping  rescue the children who are alive  this is the moment the rescuers heard a  voice under the rubble  they later find a little girl still  alive  this landslide was just one of a number  of avalanches and floods hitting the  northwest of colombia in recent weeks  dozens of families had to be relocated  near medellin on thursday  last wednesday thousands were affected  by floods when the sinu and san jorge  rivers burst their banks  experts say the heavy rains plaguing  much of colombia are due to the cyclical  la nina weather phenomenon that happens  every three to five years but its  effects are now being amplified by  climate change and with them the risk of  more tragedies like this one alessandra  al jazeera
 

